export const E1={title:'The event, EMS systems and the EMT’s role',hook:'When someone calls 911, a chain of trained people responds—dispatchers, EMTs, paramedics, emergency physicians—each working within a defined scope. The EMT is often the first clinician to reach the patient, and the decisions made in those first minutes shape everything that follows. This lesson introduces the HOSA EMT event and the system, laws and safety practices behind every call.',objectives:['Describe the HOSA EMT event, its test plan and its nine skills.','Explain EMS levels, medical direction and the EMT scope of practice.','Apply consent, refusal, confidentiality and negligence principles.','Protect yourself with standard precautions, safe lifting and stress management.'],sections:[
['The event at a glance',[
'Emergency Medical Technician is a **two-person team event**. **Round One** is a **50-question test in 60 minutes**: **Patient Assessment 20%**, **Basic Life Support 20%**, **Trauma 16%**, **Pediatrics and Childbirth 16%**, **Medical Emergencies 14%**, **Environmental Emergencies 10%** and **Special Situations 4%**. References are *Emergency Care and Transportation of the Sick and Injured* (Pollak, AAOS, 13th edition), the **AHA BLS Provider Manual** and *Emergency Care* (Limmer and O’Keefe). Skills follow the **AHA guidelines released February 2026**.',
'**Round Two** is a **12-minute** written scenario that may combine skills: **I** patient assessment—trauma, **II** patient assessment—medical, **III** BVM ventilation of an apneic adult, **IV** joint or long bone immobilization, **V** bleeding control and shock management, **VI** cardiac arrest management with AED, **VII** oxygen by non-rebreather mask, **VIII** epinephrine auto-injector and **IX** naloxone nasal spray. Teams bring a **responder bag** (gloves, masks, gowns, goggles, dressings, cravats, stethoscope and BP cuff, penlight, oral airway kit, BVM, non-rebreather, occlusive dressings) and a **manual watch with a second hand** for vital signs. Teams must reach **70%** in Round Two; ties go to the higher test score.',
{t:'table',caption:'EMT written test plan',head:['Area','Weight'],rows:[['Patient Assessment','20%'],['Basic Life Support','20%'],['Trauma','16%'],['Pediatrics and Childbirth','16%'],['Medical Emergencies','14%'],['Environmental Emergencies','10%'],['Special Situations','4%']]}]],
['The EMS system and levels of care',[
'The national EMS system is guided by the **NHTSA Office of EMS** and the **National EMS Scope of Practice Model**, which defines four licensure levels: **Emergency Medical Responder (EMR)**, **Emergency Medical Technician (EMT)**, **Advanced EMT (AEMT)** and **Paramedic**. EMTs provide basic life support: assessment, airway management with adjuncts and suction, oxygen, CPR and AED, bleeding control, splinting, childbirth assistance and a limited list of medications such as oral glucose, aspirin, epinephrine auto-injectors and naloxone. The **National Registry of EMTs (NREMT)** offers certification exams used by most states; states grant the license.',
'Every EMS agency has a **medical director**, a physician who is responsible for patient care. **Offline (indirect) medical control** includes protocols and **standing orders** written in advance; **online (direct) medical control** is real-time consultation by radio or phone. EMTs work only within their **scope of practice** and local protocols, and they practice **continuous quality improvement** through call reviews and education.',
match('EMS term and meaning',[['EMR','First level; basic immediate care'],['EMT','Basic life support and transport'],['Paramedic','Advanced life support and medications'],['Medical director','Physician responsible for EMS patient care'],['Standing orders','Written protocols used without calling'],['Online medical control','Real-time direction by radio or phone']])]],
['Consent, refusal and confidentiality',[
'**Expressed consent** is given by a competent adult, verbally or by action. **Implied consent** applies when the patient is unresponsive or unable to decide and has a life-threatening condition. **Minors** usually need a parent’s or guardian’s consent unless they are emancipated or it is an emergency. **Involuntary consent** may apply to patients under court order or police custody, following local law.',
'A competent adult may **refuse** care. The EMT must make sure the patient understands the risks, try to persuade them, offer alternatives, involve medical control and document the refusal with a signature and witness. **Advance directives** such as **DNR orders** and **POLST** forms tell EMTs which resuscitation measures the patient wants. **HIPAA** protects patient information; share it only with those involved in care, billing or as required by law. **Mandatory reporting** includes suspected child or elder abuse and certain crimes.',
sort('Type of consent',['Expressed','Implied'],[['Alert adult says “Yes, please help me”',0],['Unresponsive adult after a crash',1],['Patient holds out an arm for a BP cuff',0],['Confused patient in shock who cannot decide',1],['Alert adult nods when asked to be splinted',0]])]],
['Negligence, duty and abandonment',[
'**Negligence** requires four elements: **duty** to act, **breach** of that duty (failure to meet the standard of care), **damages** (harm to the patient) and **proximate cause** (the breach caused the harm). The **standard of care** is what a reasonably prudent EMT with similar training would do in similar circumstances. **Abandonment** is ending care without transferring the patient to someone of equal or higher training. **Assault** is placing a person in fear of harm; **battery** is touching without consent.',
'Good documentation protects patients and EMTs. The **patient care report (PCR)** records findings, vital signs, treatments, times and the patient’s response; it is a legal document and a source of data for quality improvement. If something was not documented, it is treated as not done. Correct errors with a single line, initials and date—never erase or obliterate.',
match('Legal term',[['Duty','Obligation to provide care'],['Breach','Failing to meet the standard of care'],['Damages','Harm suffered by the patient'],['Proximate cause','The breach caused the harm'],['Abandonment','Ending care without proper handoff'],['Battery','Touching a patient without consent']])]],
['Safety, lifting and well-being',[
'**Standard precautions** (body substance isolation, BSI) treat all blood and body fluids as potentially infectious: gloves for every patient, eye protection and masks for splashes or airway care, gowns for heavy contamination and an **N95** respirator for suspected tuberculosis or airborne disease. **Hand hygiene** with alcohol-based hand rub is required at the end of each HOSA skill. Scene safety comes first—never enter an unsafe scene; wait for police, fire or hazmat crews.',
'Use the **power lift** (feet shoulder-width apart, back locked, lift with the legs) and **power grip** (palms up, hands at least 10 inches apart). Keep the weight close, avoid twisting and communicate with your partner. EMS work is stressful: **critical incident stress**, burnout and post-traumatic stress are real risks. Sleep, nutrition, exercise, peer support and employee assistance programs help EMTs stay well.',
order('Power lift',['Feet shoulder-width apart','Bend at the knees and hips','Lock the back in a natural curve','Grip with palms up, hands about 10 inches apart','Lift with the legs, keeping the weight close','Communicate and move together'])]],
['Communication and the radio report',[
'EMTs communicate with dispatch, medical control, receiving hospitals and patients. A **radio report** to the hospital follows the HOSA rating sheet: **unit identification**, **patient’s age and sex**, **chief complaint**, **brief history of the current problem**, **physical findings** (general appearance, vital signs and level of consciousness), **treatment in progress**, **response to treatment** and **estimated time of arrival**. At the hospital, a verbal **handoff** repeats the key points to the receiving nurse or physician.',
'With patients, introduce yourself, use the patient’s name, position yourself at eye level, speak clearly and explain what you are doing. Use interpreters for language barriers and adapt for hearing or vision loss. Use **closed-loop communication** with your partner—repeat back instructions so nothing is missed.',
order('Radio report to the emergency department',['Unit identification','Patient’s age and sex','Chief complaint','Brief history of the current problem','Physical findings and vital signs','Treatment in progress and response','Estimated time of arrival'])]]
],takeaways:['Two-person team; test: Patient Assessment and BLS 20% each, Trauma and Pediatrics/Childbirth 16% each, Medical 14%, Environmental 10%, Special Situations 4%; nine Round Two skills in a 12-minute scenario.','EMS levels are EMR, EMT, AEMT and Paramedic; the medical director oversees care through offline protocols and online control; EMTs stay within their scope.','Consent (expressed, implied, minors), refusal with documentation, HIPAA, negligence (duty, breach, damages, cause), abandonment; standard precautions, power lift and the radio report: unit, age and sex, complaint, history, findings, treatment, response, ETA.'],terms:[['Scope of practice','The care an EMT is legally allowed to provide.'],['Medical direction','Physician oversight of EMS care through protocols and consultation.'],['Standard of care','What a reasonably prudent EMT would do in similar circumstances.']],mcqs:[q('Which level of EMS provider is above the EMT?','Advanced EMT','Emergency Medical Responder','First aider','Dispatcher','Then paramedic.'),q('Written protocols that allow care without calling a physician are called:','Standing orders','Online medical control','HIPAA','Consent','Offline control.'),q('Consent assumed for an unresponsive patient is:','Implied consent','Expressed consent','Informed refusal','Involuntary consent','Life-threatening condition.'),q('Which is NOT one of the four elements of negligence?','Consent','Duty','Breach','Damages','Plus proximate cause.'),q('Ending care without handing off to equal or higher training is:','Abandonment','Battery','Assault','Libel','Never leave.'),q('Which law protects patient health information?','HIPAA','Good Samaritan law','OSHA only','EMTALA only','Privacy.'),q('In the power lift, the EMT should:','Lift with the legs and keep the back locked','Bend at the waist','Twist while lifting','Hold the weight far away','Protect the back.'),q('What is the last item in the HOSA radio report?','Estimated time of arrival','Unit identification','Chief complaint','Age and sex','ETA.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain the EMT’s legal and safety responsibilities on a call, including consent and refusal, negligence and abandonment, standard precautions and communication with the hospital.',rubric:[['Consent and refusal',['expressed','implied','minor','refuse','competent','risks','document','signature']],['Negligence',['duty','breach','damages','cause','standard of care','abandonment']],['Safety',['scene safety','standard precautions','bsi','gloves','eye protection','hand hygiene','power lift']],['Communication',['radio report','unit','age','sex','chief complaint','treatment','eta','handoff']]],model:'Before treating, the EMT confirms scene safety and uses standard precautions—gloves for every patient, eye protection and a mask for airway care or splashes and hand hygiene afterward. A competent adult gives expressed consent, while an unresponsive patient with a life-threatening problem is treated under implied consent; minors usually need a parent’s consent unless it is an emergency. If a competent adult refuses, the EMT explains the risks, tries to persuade them, contacts medical control and documents the refusal with a signature and witness. The EMT has a duty to act and must meet the standard of care—what a reasonably prudent EMT would do—because negligence occurs when a breach of duty causes damages. Once care begins, the EMT may not leave the patient until care is transferred to someone of equal or higher training, which would otherwise be abandonment. EMTs use the power lift, keeping the back locked and lifting with the legs, to protect themselves. Finally, they communicate clearly with the hospital by radio: unit identification, the patient’s age and sex, chief complaint, brief history, physical findings and vital signs, treatment in progress, response to treatment and estimated time of arrival, followed by a verbal handoff and a complete patient care report.'},sources:[RULES,EMS_GOV,NREMT]};

export const E2={title:'Patient assessment: trauma (Skill I)',hook:'In trauma, the most obvious injury is not always the most dangerous. A screaming patient with a deformed leg may be fine compared with the quiet patient whose abdomen is filling with blood. A systematic assessment—scene size-up, primary survey, rapid trauma exam—finds and treats life threats in order. Skill I tests the whole sequence.',objectives:['Perform a scene size-up and primary survey following HOSA Skill I.','Use AVPU and the airway, breathing and circulation checks to find life threats.','Perform a rapid trauma assessment using DCAP-BTLS.','Obtain a SAMPLE history, vital signs and a hospital report.'],sections:[
['Scene size-up: Skill I steps 1–2',[
'**1. Scene size-up:** **a.** determine the **scene is safe**; **b.** determine the **mechanism of injury (MOI)** or nature of illness; **c.** determine the **number of patients**; **d.** **request additional EMS assistance** if needed. Look for hazards—traffic, fire, downed lines, hazardous materials, unstable vehicles, violence—and use **standard precautions** throughout. **2.** If trauma is suspected, verbalize, “**Spinal motion restriction performed at this time**,” with a partner holding manual stabilization of the head.',
'**Mechanism of injury** predicts hidden injuries. **Significant MOI** includes ejection from a vehicle, death of another occupant, falls greater than **20 feet** for adults (or 10 feet or two to three times the height for children), rollover crashes, high-speed collisions, pedestrian or bicycle struck by a vehicle and penetrating wounds of the head, chest or abdomen. **Blunt trauma** spreads force over an area; **penetrating trauma** follows the path of the object.',
sort('Significant mechanism of injury?',['Significant MOI','Lower-risk MOI'],[['Ejection from a vehicle',0],['Fall of 25 feet',0],['Twisted ankle stepping off a curb',1],['Pedestrian struck by a car',0],['Small cut from a kitchen knife on a finger',1],['Rollover crash',0]])]],
['Primary survey: Skill I steps 3–7',[
'**3. Primary survey:** **a.** verbalize a **general impression** (age, sex, position, obvious injuries, how sick the patient looks); **b.** determine **level of consciousness** with **AVPU**—**A**lert, responds to **V**erbal stimuli, responds to **P**ain, **U**nresponsive; **c.** determine the **chief complaint and apparent life threats**. **4. Airway:** open (jaw thrust for trauma) and maintain it. **5. Breathing:** assess **rate, rhythm and volume**, assure adequate ventilation, start **appropriate oxygen** and manage injuries that compromise breathing, such as an open chest wound.',
'**6. Circulation:** check the **pulse**, assess **skin color, temperature or condition**, find and **control major bleeding** and begin **shock management** (position the patient, conserve body heat, give oxygen). Many systems now address **massive external hemorrhage first** (X-ABC) because it can kill faster than an airway problem. **7.** Identify **patient priority** and make **treatment and transport decisions**: high-priority patients—poor general impression, unresponsive, difficulty breathing, shock, uncontrolled bleeding, severe pain—need rapid transport to an appropriate facility such as a **trauma center**.',
order('Primary survey',['General impression','Level of consciousness (AVPU)','Chief complaint and life threats','Airway','Breathing and oxygen','Circulation, bleeding and shock','Priority and transport decision'])]],
['Rapid trauma assessment and DCAP-BTLS: Skill I step 8',[
'The **rapid trauma assessment** is a head-to-toe exam for patients with significant MOI or altered mental status. At each area, look and feel for **DCAP-BTLS**: **D**eformities, **C**ontusions, **A**brasions, **P**unctures/penetrations, **B**urns, **T**enderness, **L**acerations and **S**welling. Check the **head** (plus crepitation), **face**, **ears and nose** (blood or clear fluid), **eyes** (discoloration, unequal pupils, foreign bodies, blood in the anterior chamber) and **mouth** (loose or broken teeth, obstructions, swelling, unusual breath odor).',
'Check the **neck** for **jugular vein distention (JVD)** and crepitation, then apply a **cervical collar**. Inspect and palpate the **chest** for crepitation, **paradoxical motion**, retractions and work of breathing, and **auscultate** breath sounds for presence, absence and equality. Check the **abdomen** (firm, soft, distended), **pelvis** with gentle compression, **upper and lower extremities** including **circulation, sensation and motor function (CSM/PMS)**, then **roll the patient with spinal precautions** to check the **posterior**.',
flip('DCAP-BTLS',[['D','Deformities'],['C','Contusions'],['A','Abrasions'],['P','Punctures or penetrations'],['B','Burns'],['T','Tenderness'],['L','Lacerations'],['S','Swelling']])]],
['History and vital signs: Skill I steps 9–12',[
'**9. SAMPLE history:** **S**igns and symptoms, **A**llergies, **M**edications, **P**ertinent past medical history, **L**ast oral intake and **E**vents leading to the present illness or injury. **10.** Obtain **baseline vital signs**—the rating sheet requires **blood pressure, pulse and respirations**—plus skin, pupils and pulse oximetry when available. Repeat them to find **trends**.',
'**11.** Manage **secondary injuries and wounds** (dressings, splints) as time allows—never delay transport of a critical patient for minor injuries. **12.** **Reassess** every **5 minutes** for unstable patients and every **15 minutes** for stable patients, repeating the primary survey, vital signs, focused assessment and checks of interventions. **13.** Verbalize interventions and the **destination**, and **14.** communicate with the patient and your partner throughout.',
flip('SAMPLE',[['S','Signs and symptoms'],['A','Allergies'],['M','Medications'],['P','Pertinent past history'],['L','Last oral intake'],['E','Events leading up']])]],
['The hospital report and wrap-up: Skill I steps 15–17',[
'**15.** Give a report to the emergency department (judge) with **unit identification**, the **patient’s age and sex**, **chief complaint**, **brief history**, **treatment in progress**, **response to treatment** and **estimated time of arrival**. **16.** Use **alcohol-based hand rub**. **17.** Practice **body substance isolation** throughout. Skill I has the most points of any EMT skill, so practice the order until it is automatic and say every step aloud.',
'Teams often divide work: one partner holds manual stabilization and manages the airway while the other performs the exam and vital signs, then they switch tasks as needed. Ask the judge for findings (“Do I hear breath sounds?” “Is there a distal pulse?”) and treat what you find immediately—an open chest wound gets an occlusive dressing during the primary survey, not at the end.',
order('Trauma assessment overview',['Scene size-up and spinal motion restriction','Primary survey and life threats','Priority and transport decision','Rapid trauma assessment (DCAP-BTLS)','SAMPLE history and baseline vital signs','Reassess and report to the hospital'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Motorcycle crash',{a:{text:'A motorcyclist was thrown 30 feet and is moaning, responding only to pain. (Scenario invented for practice.) After scene safety and BSI, what do you do?',choices:[['Verbalize spinal motion restriction and begin the primary survey','b','Correct. Significant MOI.',true],['Check the deformed wrist first','b','Find life threats first.',false]]},b:{text:'Breathing is 32 and shallow, with blood spurting from the thigh. What next?',choices:[['Control the bleeding, give oxygen and plan rapid transport','c','Correct. High priority.',true],['Take a full SAMPLE history first','c','Treat life threats first.',false]]},c:{text:'You complete a rapid trauma assessment en route and report to the trauma center.',end:true}})]],
["Common errors in the trauma assessment",["The most frequent point losses in Skill I come from **doing steps out of order**—for example, splinting an ankle before checking breathing—**forgetting to verbalize spinal motion restriction**, **not controlling major bleeding during the primary survey** and **omitting a baseline blood pressure**. Another error is skipping the **posterior** check: the team must log-roll with spinal precautions to inspect the back. Judges also look for **body substance isolation throughout** and hand rub at the end.","Build a routine with your partner: one person always calls out each primary-survey letter, and the other confirms what was found and done. Treat as you go—an occlusive dressing for an open chest wound, oxygen for poor breathing, a tourniquet for spurting bleeding—then continue the exam. Finish with the **transport decision** before the detailed exam if the patient is critical.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Correct order in Skill I?","buckets":["Do early (scene and primary survey)","Do later (secondary steps)"],"items":[["Determine number of patients",0],["Control spurting bleeding",0],["Check the posterior with a log roll",1],["Assess breathing and give oxygen",0],["Obtain a SAMPLE history",1],["Bandage minor abrasions",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['Scene size-up: safety, MOI, number of patients, more resources; verbalize spinal motion restriction for suspected trauma; significant MOI includes ejection, falls over 20 feet, rollovers and pedestrians struck.','Primary survey: general impression, AVPU, chief complaint, airway, breathing with oxygen, circulation with bleeding control and shock care, then priority and transport decision.','Rapid trauma assessment with DCAP-BTLS head to toe, JVD, breath sounds, pelvis, CSM in extremities, log roll for the back; SAMPLE, baseline BP, pulse and respirations; reassess every 5 minutes unstable, 15 stable; report to the ED.'],terms:[['Mechanism of injury','How the force of an injury was applied to the body.'],['AVPU','Alert, verbal, pain, unresponsive—a scale for level of consciousness.'],['DCAP-BTLS','Deformities, contusions, abrasions, punctures, burns, tenderness, lacerations, swelling.']],mcqs:[q('What does the P in AVPU stand for?','Responds to pain','Pulse','Pupils','Position','Alert, verbal, pain, unresponsive.'),q('Which is a significant mechanism of injury in an adult?','A fall greater than 20 feet','A paper cut','A sprained finger','A bee sting','Predicts hidden injury.'),q('What is checked in the neck during a rapid trauma assessment?','Jugular vein distention and crepitation','Pupils','Capillary refill','Breath odor','Then apply a collar.'),q('What does the L in SAMPLE stand for?','Last oral intake','Lungs','Level of consciousness','Lacerations','Food or drink.'),q('How often are unstable patients reassessed?','Every 5 minutes','Every 15 minutes','Every hour','Only at the hospital','Stable: every 15.'),q('Which vital signs must be included in the baseline per the HOSA rating sheet?','Blood pressure, pulse and respirations','Temperature only','Blood glucose only','Pupils only','BP, P and R.'),q('Paradoxical chest motion suggests:','Flail chest','Normal breathing','Asthma','Diabetes','Check the chest.'),q('In trauma, the airway is opened with the:','Jaw-thrust maneuver','Head-tilt only','Neck flexion','Rolling the head','Protects the spine.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe the HOSA Skill I trauma assessment from scene size-up through the hospital report.',rubric:[['Scene size-up',['safe','mechanism','number of patients','additional','spinal motion restriction']],['Primary survey',['general impression','avpu','chief complaint','airway','breathing','oxygen','pulse','skin','bleeding','shock','priority']],['Rapid trauma assessment',['dcap-btls','head','neck','jvd','collar','chest','breath sounds','abdomen','pelvis','extremities','circulation','sensation','motor','posterior']],['History, vitals and report',['sample','blood pressure','pulse','respirations','reassess','unit','age','chief complaint','eta']]],model:'We begin with a scene size-up: confirming the scene is safe, identifying the mechanism of injury, counting the patients and calling for more resources if needed, while using body substance isolation. Because trauma is suspected, my partner holds manual stabilization and I verbalize that spinal motion restriction is being performed. In the primary survey I state a general impression, check the level of consciousness with AVPU and identify the chief complaint and life threats. I open the airway with a jaw thrust and maintain it, assess breathing rate, rhythm and volume, give oxygen and manage any chest injury. I check the pulse and skin, control major bleeding and begin shock care, then decide the patient’s priority and transport destination. Next, I perform a rapid trauma assessment head to toe, looking and feeling for DCAP-BTLS—checking the head, face, ears, eyes, nose and mouth, the neck for JVD and crepitation before applying a cervical collar, the chest for paradoxical motion with breath sounds, the abdomen, the pelvis with gentle compression and all four extremities for circulation, sensation and motor function, then log-rolling to check the back. I obtain a SAMPLE history and baseline blood pressure, pulse and respirations, treat secondary injuries, reassess every 5 minutes if unstable and give the hospital a report with unit identification, age and sex, chief complaint, history, treatment, response and ETA.'},sources:[RULES,EMS_GOV,NREMT]};

export const E3={title:'Patient assessment: medical and vital signs (Skill II)',hook:'Medical patients often tell you what is wrong—if you ask the right questions. A careful history explains most medical complaints, and vital signs show how well the body is coping. Skill II tests the medical assessment, including OPQRST, SAMPLE and a focused exam of the affected body system.',objectives:['Perform a medical assessment following HOSA Skill II.','Use OPQRST and SAMPLE to obtain a history.','Measure and interpret vital signs, including blood pressure.','Choose a focused secondary assessment by body system.'],sections:[
['Scene size-up and primary survey: Skill II steps 1–5',[
'**1.** Scene size-up: safety, **nature of illness**, number of patients and need for additional resources. Clues include medication bottles, oxygen equipment, smells, and what family or bystanders say. **2.** Primary survey: **general impression**, **AVPU** and **chief complaint and life threats**. **3.** Airway and breathing: assess **rate, rhythm and volume**, assure adequate ventilation and start **appropriate oxygen**. **4.** Circulation: control **major bleeding**, check the **pulse** and assess **skin color, temperature and condition**.',
'**5.** Identify **priority** and make the **transport decision**. Responsive medical patients usually get a **focused history first**, then a physical exam of the affected system; unresponsive medical patients get a **rapid head-to-toe exam** first because they cannot give a history. Ask about advance directives when appropriate and gather medications to bring to the hospital.',
order('Medical assessment overview',['Scene size-up and nature of illness','General impression, AVPU, chief complaint','Airway, breathing and oxygen','Circulation and skin','Priority and transport decision','OPQRST and SAMPLE history','Focused secondary assessment and vital signs'])]],
['OPQRST: Skill II step 6',[
'**OPQRST** explores the chief complaint, especially pain or shortness of breath: **O**nset—what were you doing when it started; was it sudden or gradual? **P**rovokes/palliation—what makes it better or worse? **Q**uality—what does it feel like (sharp, dull, crushing, tearing)? **R**adiation—does it move anywhere? **S**everity—rate it from 0 to 10. **T**ime—when did it start and has it changed? Then ask **clarifying questions** about associated signs and symptoms, such as nausea, sweating or dizziness with chest pain.',
'Use **open-ended questions** (“Tell me about your pain”) before closed questions (“Is it sharp?”), and record **pertinent negatives**—important symptoms the patient denies, such as “denies shortness of breath.” Listen without interrupting, and use family members for history when the patient cannot answer.',
flip('OPQRST',[['O','Onset—what were you doing?'],['P','Provokes or palliates—better or worse?'],['Q','Quality—what does it feel like?'],['R','Radiation—does it spread?'],['S','Severity—0 to 10'],['T','Time—when did it start?']])]],
['SAMPLE and the secondary assessment: Skill II steps 7–8',[
'**7. Past medical history** uses **SAMPLE**: signs and symptoms, allergies, medications, pertinent medical history, last oral intake and events leading to the present illness. Ask about prescription, over-the-counter and herbal medications and recent changes. **8. Secondary assessment** examines the **affected body part or system**: **cardiovascular** (chest pain, pulses, edema), **neurological** (mental status, pupils, stroke scale, glucose), **integumentary** (skin, rashes, wounds), **reproductive** (pregnancy, bleeding), **pulmonary** (breath sounds, work of breathing), **musculoskeletal**, **GI/GU** (abdominal pain, vomiting, urination) and **psychological/social**.',
'A focused exam saves time: a patient with shortness of breath needs breath sounds, pulse oximetry, accessory muscle use and position, while a patient with abdominal pain needs inspection, palpation of the four quadrants (painful area last) and questions about vomiting, stool and urine.',
match('Complaint and focused exam',[['Chest pain','Cardiovascular—pulses, skin, BP'],['Slurred speech','Neurological—stroke scale, glucose'],['Shortness of breath','Pulmonary—breath sounds, SpO₂'],['Abdominal pain','GI/GU—palpate four quadrants'],['Rash after a new medication','Integumentary—skin'],['Anxiety and hopelessness','Psychological/social']])]],
['Vital signs: Skill II step 9',[
'**Baseline vital signs** must include **blood pressure, pulse and respirations**. **Systolic pressure** is the pressure created when the heart **contracts** and forces blood into the arteries; **diastolic pressure** is the pressure when the heart is **relaxing**. Normal adult values are roughly a **pulse of 60–100**, **respirations of 12–20**, **blood pressure below about 120/80** and **SpO₂ of 94–99%**. Count the pulse and respirations for 30 seconds and multiply by 2 (a full minute if irregular), using a watch with a second hand.',
'Also assess **skin** (color, temperature, moisture; capillary refill under 2 seconds in children), **pupils** (equal, round, reactive to light), **blood glucose** when indicated and **temperature**. A single reading tells you little—**trends** in repeated vital signs reveal whether the patient is improving or deteriorating.',
{t:'w',kind:'vitalsreport',title:'Record or report?',prompt:'Decide which readings fall within the usual adult teaching ranges and which you would report to medical control.',person:'patient',reportTo:'medical control'}]],
['Reassessment and the medical report: Skill II steps 10–16',[
'**10.** Manage secondary injuries. **11.** **Reassess**: every **5 minutes** if unstable, **15 minutes** if stable—repeat the primary survey, vital signs, focused exam and check interventions such as oxygen flow. **12.** Verbalize **interventions and destination** (for example, a stroke center or a hospital capable of cardiac catheterization). **13.** Communicate with the patient and partner.',
'**14.** The medical report adds **physical findings**: unit identification, age and sex, chief complaint, brief history, **physical findings including general appearance, vital signs and level of consciousness**, treatment in progress, response to treatment and **ETA**. **15.** Use hand rub, and **16.** practice BSI throughout. Skill II has 92 points; mastery is 64.4.',
chk('Which statement best describes systolic blood pressure?',['The pressure created when the heart contracts and forces blood into the artery','An amount that is double the diastolic pressure','The difference between resting and pumping pressure','The pressure when the heart is relaxing'],'HOSA sample question. Diastolic is the relaxing pressure.'),
kase('Chest pain at home',{a:{text:'A 62-year-old man reports chest pressure that began 30 minutes ago while mowing the lawn. (Scenario invented for practice.) Which question explores radiation?',choices:[['“Does the pain move anywhere, like your arm or jaw?”','b','Correct. R in OPQRST.',true],['“What did you eat last?”','b','That is L in SAMPLE.',false]]},b:{text:'Vital signs: BP 150/92, pulse 104, respirations 22, SpO₂ 93%. What is a priority?',choices:[['Give oxygen as indicated and plan prompt transport to a cardiac-capable hospital','c','Correct.',true],['Wait 30 minutes and recheck','c','Chest pain is high priority.',false]]},c:{text:'You reassess every 5 minutes and give the ED a report with physical findings and ETA.',end:true}})]],
["Assessing special populations",["**Older adults** may have atypical presentations—a heart attack without chest pain, infection without fever, or confusion as the only sign of illness. They often take many medications (**polypharmacy**), may have hearing or vision loss and may have fragile skin and bones. Ask about baseline mental status from family or caregivers and look for clues of **elder abuse or neglect**.","Patients with **developmental disabilities**, **dementia** or **mental health crises** may need more time, simple language and a calm environment. For patients who speak another language, use a professional interpreter or language line. **Bariatric** patients need appropriately sized BP cuffs and lifting equipment. Always respect privacy, explain each step and ask permission before touching.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Population and assessment tip","pairs":[["Older adult","Ask family about baseline mental status"],["Many medications","Watch for polypharmacy effects"],["Dementia","Simple language and a calm setting"],["Language barrier","Use a professional interpreter"],["Bariatric patient","Correct-size cuff and lifting equipment"],["Hearing loss","Face the patient and speak clearly"]]}]],
["Measuring blood pressure and other signs accurately",["To measure BP by **auscultation**, choose a cuff whose bladder covers about **80% of the arm’s circumference**, wrap it about 1 inch above the elbow crease, palpate the brachial artery, inflate about 30 mm Hg past where the radial pulse disappears and deflate at about **2–3 mm Hg per second**. The first sound is the **systolic** pressure and the point where sounds disappear is the **diastolic** pressure. **Palpation** gives only a systolic reading and is useful in noisy settings.","A cuff that is **too small** gives falsely **high** readings; one that is **too large** gives falsely **low** readings. **Pulse pressure** is systolic minus diastolic—narrowing pulse pressure can signal shock or cardiac tamponade. Check **orthostatic** (position) changes only if protocol allows and the patient is stable. Always compare vital signs over time.",{"t":"check","q":"A cuff that is too small for the patient’s arm will usually give a reading that is:","options":["Falsely high","Falsely low","Accurate","Impossible to hear"],"why":"Choose a bladder that covers about 80% of the arm."}]]
],takeaways:['Medical assessment: scene size-up and nature of illness, primary survey, priority; responsive patients get a focused history first, unresponsive patients get a rapid exam first.','OPQRST (onset, provokes, quality, radiation, severity, time) plus SAMPLE; focused secondary assessment by system: cardiovascular, neurological, integumentary, reproductive, pulmonary, musculoskeletal, GI/GU, psychological.','Systolic = pressure during contraction; normal adult pulse 60–100, respirations 12–20, BP under about 120/80, SpO₂ 94–99%; reassess every 5 or 15 minutes; report includes physical findings, vitals and LOC.'],terms:[['OPQRST','Onset, provokes, quality, radiation, severity, time—questions about a complaint.'],['Systolic pressure','Pressure in the arteries when the heart contracts.'],['Pertinent negative','An important symptom the patient denies.']],mcqs:[q('Which statement best describes the systolic blood pressure?','The pressure created when the heart contracts and forces blood into the artery','An amount that is double the diastolic pressure','The difference between the resting pressure and the pumping pressure','The pressure when the heart is relaxing and allowing blood into the atria','HOSA sample question.'),q('What does the Q in OPQRST stand for?','Quality','Quantity','Quickness','Questions','What does it feel like?'),q('A normal adult respiratory rate is:','12–20 per minute','4–8 per minute','30–40 per minute','60–100 per minute','At rest.'),q('A normal adult pulse is:','60–100 per minute','30–50 per minute','120–160 per minute','10–20 per minute','At rest.'),q('An unresponsive medical patient should first receive:','A rapid head-to-toe exam','A long OPQRST interview','Nothing until arrival','Only a glucose check','They cannot give a history.'),q('Which system is examined for slurred speech?','Neurological','Integumentary','Reproductive','Musculoskeletal','Stroke scale.'),q('A symptom the patient denies that is important to record is a:','Pertinent negative','Chief complaint','Mechanism of injury','Standing order','Example: no shortness of breath.'),q('Normal SpO₂ is about:','94–99%','70–80%','50%','100–110%','Pulse oximetry.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how an EMT assesses a responsive patient with a medical complaint following HOSA Skill II, including the history, secondary assessment, vital signs and report.',rubric:[['Scene and primary',['scene','nature of illness','general impression','avpu','airway','breathing','oxygen','pulse','skin','priority']],['History',['opqrst','onset','provokes','quality','radiation','severity','time','sample','allergies','medications']],['Exam and vitals',['secondary','system','cardiovascular','neurological','pulmonary','blood pressure','pulse','respirations','systolic']],['Reassessment and report',['reassess','5 minutes','15 minutes','unit','age','physical findings','vital signs','level of consciousness','eta']]],model:'After confirming scene safety, determining the nature of illness and the number of patients and calling for help if needed, I form a general impression, check the level of consciousness with AVPU and identify the chief complaint and any life threats. I assess airway and breathing—rate, rhythm and volume—start oxygen if indicated, check for major bleeding, the pulse and skin and decide the patient’s priority and destination. Because the patient is responsive, I take the history first using OPQRST: onset, what provokes or relieves it, its quality, radiation, severity on a 0–10 scale and time, plus clarifying questions about associated symptoms, followed by SAMPLE—signs and symptoms, allergies, medications, pertinent history, last oral intake and events leading up. I then perform a focused secondary assessment of the affected system, such as cardiovascular, neurological, pulmonary or GI/GU, and obtain baseline vital signs including blood pressure, pulse and respirations, remembering that systolic pressure is the pressure when the heart contracts. I reassess every 5 minutes if unstable or 15 minutes if stable and give the emergency department a report with unit identification, age and sex, chief complaint, brief history, physical findings including general appearance, vital signs and level of consciousness, treatment, response and ETA, then use hand rub.'},sources:[RULES,MEDLINE_VS,MEDLINE_BP,NREMT]};

export const E4={title:'Airway, ventilation and oxygen (Skills III and VII)',hook:'Without an open airway and adequate breathing, nothing else matters—brain cells begin to die within minutes without oxygen. EMTs suction, insert airway adjuncts, ventilate with a bag-valve mask and give oxygen. Skill III tests the apneic adult with a pulse, and Skill VII tests oxygen by non-rebreather mask.',objectives:['Recognize adequate and inadequate breathing.','Suction and insert an oropharyngeal airway following HOSA Skill III.','Ventilate an apneic patient with a BVM at the correct rate.','Prepare an oxygen cylinder and apply a non-rebreather mask following HOSA Skill VII.'],sections:[
['Adequate and inadequate breathing',[
'**Adequate breathing** has a normal rate (adults 12–20), regular rhythm, good chest rise (tidal volume), equal breath sounds and no unusual effort. **Inadequate breathing** includes rates that are too fast or too slow, shallow breaths, **accessory muscle use**, retractions, nasal flaring, tripod positioning, **cyanosis**, altered mental status and noisy breathing. **Agonal gasps** are not breathing. Breath sounds include **wheezing** (narrowed lower airways), **crackles (rales)** (fluid), **rhonchi** (mucus) and **stridor** (upper airway obstruction).',
'Patients who are **breathing adequately but hypoxic** need oxygen; patients who are **breathing inadequately** need **assisted ventilation** with a BVM. The **jaw-thrust** maneuver opens the airway when spinal injury is suspected; the **head-tilt/chin-lift** is used otherwise. Pulse oximetry helps, but trust your assessment—SpO₂ can be falsely normal in carbon monoxide poisoning.',
sort('Breathing sound and meaning',['Upper airway','Lower airway or lungs'],[['Stridor',0],['Wheezing',1],['Crackles (rales)',1],['Snoring',0],['Rhonchi',1]])]],
['Suctioning: Skill III steps 1–10',[
'**1–3.** Check scene safety, responsiveness and **breathing and pulse together for 5–10 seconds**. The judge states the patient is **unresponsive and apneic** with a **weak pulse of 60** and a mouth full of **secretions and vomitus**. **4.** Request additional EMS. **5.** Turn on the suction unit and **test it (more than 300 mm Hg)**. **6.** **Measure the catheter** from the **corner of the mouth to the earlobe** or angle of the jaw. **7.** Turn the head to the side (unless cervical spine injury is suspected).',
'**8.** Open the mouth with the **cross-finger technique**. **9.** **Insert the catheter to the premeasured depth without applying suction**. **10.** **Apply suction in a circular motion as you withdraw**. Limit suctioning to about **15 seconds in adults** (less in children and infants) because the patient is not being ventilated. A **rigid (Yankauer) tip** is used for the mouth and throat; a soft **French** catheter for the nose or a stoma.',
order('Suctioning',['Turn on and test suction (>300 mm Hg)','Measure catheter: corner of mouth to earlobe','Turn the head to the side if no spinal injury','Open the mouth with the cross-finger technique','Insert without suction','Suction while withdrawing in a circular motion'])]],
['Airway adjuncts: Skill III steps 11–13',[
'**11.** Open the airway with a **head tilt–chin lift** or **jaw thrust**. **12–13.** **Oropharyngeal airway (OPA):** measure from the **corner of the mouth to the tip of the earlobe** on the same side, open the mouth with the crossed-finger technique, insert with the **tip pointing to the roof of the mouth**, then **rotate 180 degrees** until the **flange rests against the lips**. An OPA is used only in patients **without a gag reflex**; if the patient gags, remove it. In infants and children, insert it with a tongue depressor without rotating.',
'**Nasopharyngeal airway (NPA):** measure from the **tip of the nose to the earlobe**, lubricate with water-soluble gel and insert with the **bevel toward the septum** into the larger nostril. NPAs are tolerated by patients with a gag reflex but are avoided with suspected **skull base fractures** (clear fluid from the nose or ears, raccoon eyes).',
order('OPA insertion',['Measure from the corner of the mouth to the earlobe','Open the mouth with crossed fingers','Insert with the tip toward the roof of the mouth','Rotate 180 degrees','Flange rests against the lips','Remove if the patient gags'])]],
['BVM ventilation: Skill III steps 14–21',[
'**14.** **Ventilate immediately** with a BVM (it may be attached to oxygen if the first ventilation is delivered within **30 seconds**). **15.** **Recheck the pulse** for no more than 10 seconds. **16.** Attach the BVM to oxygen at **15 L/min**. **17.** Ventilate adequately: **a.** enough **volume to make the chest rise**; **b.** squeeze the bag **once every 6 seconds** for an adult (**10 breaths per minute**). **18.** Start ventilation within **30 seconds** of BSI and never interrupt ventilations for more than **30 seconds**.',
'Use the **E-C clamp** to seal the mask; two-rescuer BVM is more effective—one seals the mask with both hands while the other squeezes. Signs of good ventilation are chest rise, improving skin color and SpO₂ and a heart rate that returns to normal. Ventilating **too fast or too forcefully** causes gastric distension and vomiting and reduces blood return to the heart. For **children and infants**, give **1 breath every 2–3 seconds**.',
flip('Ventilation numbers',[['Adult with a pulse','1 breath every 6 seconds (10/min)'],['Child or infant with a pulse','1 breath every 2–3 seconds'],['BVM oxygen flow','15 L/min'],['First breath','Within 30 seconds'],['Max interruption','30 seconds'],['Pulse recheck','No more than 10 seconds']])]],
['Oxygen delivery: Skill VII',[
'Oxygen cylinders are labeled and color-coded **green** in the United States; a **D cylinder** holds about 350 L, and a full cylinder is about **2,000 psi**. Replace a cylinder at the **safe residual pressure of about 200 psi**. Keep cylinders away from heat and flames and secure them so they cannot fall. Skill VII (tank empty; steps simulated): **1–2.** Scene safety and equipment. **3.** Use a wrench to turn the valve **counterclockwise to “crack”** the tank briefly, removing dust, then **4.** retighten. **5.** Attach the **regulator** using the **pin-indexing holes**, with the **washer** over the larger hole, hand-tighten, then open the tank fully.',
'**6.** Check the **tank pressure**. **7.** Check for **leaks**. **8.** Attach the **non-rebreather** to the correct port. **9.** Turn on the flow to **pre-fill the reservoir bag**. **10.** Set the flow to **at least 10 L/min** (commonly 10–15). **11.** Apply the mask and adjust to fit snugly. A **non-rebreather** delivers up to about **90% oxygen**; a **nasal cannula** at **1–6 L/min** delivers about 24–44% for patients who need less or cannot tolerate a mask. Patients who are breathing inadequately need a BVM, not a non-rebreather.',
order('Non-rebreather setup',['Crack the valve and retighten','Attach the regulator with the pin-index system and washer','Open the tank and check pressure','Check for leaks','Attach the mask and pre-fill the reservoir','Set at least 10 L/min and fit the mask snugly'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Overdose with vomiting',{a:{text:'An unresponsive adult has a weak pulse of 60, no breathing and vomit in the mouth. (Scenario invented for practice.) What first?',choices:[['Test suction, measure the catheter and suction while withdrawing','b','Correct. Clear the airway.',true],['Start BVM breaths through the vomit','b','You would force vomit into the lungs.',false]]},b:{text:'The mouth is clear and there is no gag reflex. What next?',choices:[['Insert a measured OPA and ventilate with a BVM, 1 breath every 6 seconds','c','Correct.',true],['Apply a non-rebreather at 10 L/min','c','An apneic patient needs ventilation.',false]]},c:{text:'You attach oxygen at 15 L/min and recheck the pulse while ventilating.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Inadequate breathing (abnormal rate, shallow, accessory muscles, cyanosis, altered mental status) needs BVM ventilation; adequate breathing with hypoxia needs oxygen; stridor is upper airway, wheezes and crackles are lower.','Skill III: test suction (>300 mm Hg), measure corner of mouth to earlobe, insert without suction, suction while withdrawing; OPA tip to the roof then rotate 180° (no gag reflex); BVM within 30 seconds, 15 L/min, 1 breath every 6 seconds.','Skill VII: crack the valve, pin-index regulator with washer, check pressure and leaks, pre-fill the reservoir, at least 10 L/min; non-rebreather up to about 90%, nasal cannula 1–6 L/min.'],terms:[['Oropharyngeal airway','A curved device that keeps the tongue from blocking the airway.'],['Bag-valve mask','A hand-squeezed device for ventilating a patient.'],['Non-rebreather mask','A mask with a reservoir bag that delivers high-concentration oxygen.']],mcqs:[q('If an adult patient is not breathing but has a pulse, the patient should be ventilated at a rate of how many breaths per minute?','10','6','14','18','HOSA sample question.'),q('How is a suction catheter measured?','From the corner of the mouth to the earlobe','From the nose to the chin','From the chin to the chest','It is not measured','Or angle of the jaw.'),q('When is suction applied?','While withdrawing the catheter','While inserting it','Continuously for a minute','Never','Circular motion.'),q('An OPA is inserted with the tip toward the roof of the mouth and then:','Rotated 180 degrees','Pulled out','Left pointing up','Pushed into the nose','Flange at the lips.'),q('An OPA should not be used if the patient:','Has a gag reflex','Is unresponsive','Is apneic','Has a pulse','Gagging can cause vomiting.'),q('BVM oxygen flow per Skill III is:','15 L/min','2 L/min','6 L/min','30 L/min','Attached to oxygen.'),q('A non-rebreather mask flow should be at least:','10 L/min','1 L/min','4 L/min','25 L/min','Pre-fill the reservoir.'),q('Which sound suggests upper airway obstruction?','Stridor','Wheezing','Crackles','Rhonchi','High-pitched.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how an EMT manages an unresponsive, apneic adult with a pulse and vomit in the airway following HOSA Skill III, and how Skill VII oxygen therapy differs.',rubric:[['Assessment',['scene','responsiveness','breathing','pulse','5','10 seconds','apneic']],['Suction',['test','300','measure','earlobe','turn the head','cross-finger','without suction','withdraw','circular']],['Adjunct and BVM',['opa','roof','180','flange','gag','bvm','30 seconds','15 l','6 seconds','chest rise']],['Oxygen therapy',['non-rebreather','breathing adequately','pre-fill','10 l','regulator','pin','leak','nasal cannula']]],model:'I verify scene safety, check responsiveness and assess breathing and the pulse together for 5–10 seconds, finding an apneic patient with a weak pulse and vomit in the mouth, and I request additional EMS. I turn on the suction and test it to more than 300 mm Hg, measure the rigid catheter from the corner of the mouth to the earlobe, turn the head to the side because no spinal injury is suspected, open the mouth with the cross-finger technique, insert the catheter to the measured depth without suction and apply suction in a circular motion while withdrawing, limiting it to about 15 seconds. Once the airway is clear, I open it with a head tilt–chin lift, measure an oropharyngeal airway from the corner of the mouth to the earlobe, insert it with the tip pointing to the roof of the mouth and rotate it 180 degrees until the flange rests on the lips, since there is no gag reflex. I ventilate with a BVM within 30 seconds, recheck the pulse, attach oxygen at 15 L/min and give one breath every 6 seconds with just enough volume to make the chest rise, never interrupting ventilation for more than 30 seconds. Skill VII is different because it is for a patient who is breathing adequately but needs oxygen: I crack the cylinder valve, attach the regulator using the pin-index holes and washer, open the tank, check the pressure and for leaks, pre-fill the non-rebreather reservoir, set the flow to at least 10 L/min and fit the mask snugly.'},sources:[RULES,AHA_CPR,NREMT]};

export const E5={title:'Cardiac emergencies and cardiac arrest with AED (Skill VI)',hook:'Heart disease is the leading cause of death in the United States. EMTs treat chest pain before it becomes cardiac arrest and run high-performance CPR when it does. Skill VI tests two-partner cardiac arrest management with an AED, BVM and airway adjunct—and when to transport.',objectives:['Recognize acute coronary syndrome and heart failure and give EMT-level care.','Manage cardiac arrest with an AED and BVM following HOSA Skill VI.','Apply transport criteria and post-arrest care.','Recognize hypertensive emergencies and other cardiovascular problems.'],sections:[
['Acute coronary syndrome and heart failure',[
'**Acute coronary syndrome (ACS)** includes **angina** (chest pain from too little blood flow, relieved by rest or nitroglycerin) and **acute myocardial infarction (AMI)** (death of heart muscle from a blocked coronary artery). Typical signs are **chest pressure or squeezing** that may radiate to the arm, jaw or back, **shortness of breath**, **diaphoresis**, nausea and anxiety. **Women, older adults and people with diabetes** may have atypical symptoms such as fatigue, indigestion or shortness of breath alone.',
'EMT care: position of comfort, oxygen if **SpO₂ is below 94%** or the patient is short of breath, **aspirin 162–324 mg chewed** if not allergic and protocol allows, and assisting with the patient’s prescribed **nitroglycerin** if systolic BP is above about 100 mm Hg and they have not taken erectile-dysfunction drugs recently. Transport to a hospital that can open blocked arteries. **Congestive heart failure** causes shortness of breath, crackles, swollen ankles and JVD; sit the patient up, give oxygen and transport.',
sort('Typical heart attack sign?',['Common sign of ACS','Not typical'],[['Crushing chest pressure radiating to the left arm',0],['Shortness of breath and sweating',0],['Pain relieved by pressing on a bruise',1],['Nausea and anxiety',0],['Fatigue and indigestion in an older woman',0],['Itchy rash on the leg',1]])]],
['Starting the resuscitation: Skill VI steps 1–7',[
'**1.** Verify **scene safety**. **2.** **Question bystanders** (what happened, how long ago, was CPR started). **3.** Partner 1 **taps the shoulder and shouts, “Are you OK?”** **4.** Shouts to Partner 2 to **initiate AED/defibrillator use**. **5.** Request **additional EMS** (advanced life support). **6.** Check **breathing and the carotid pulse together for 5–10 seconds**, scanning the chest for rise and fall and sliding **2–3 fingers into the groove between the trachea and neck muscles**. The judge states the patient is **unresponsive, apneic and pulseless**.',
'**7.** Partner 1 gives **30 initial compressions**: at the patient’s side, bulky clothing removed, patient **face up on a firm, flat surface**, heel of one hand on the **lower half of the breastbone** with the other on top, **arms straight** and shoulders over the hands, **100–120/min (30 in 15–18 seconds)**, **at least 2 inches (5 cm)** deep, counting aloud and allowing **full recoil**.',
order('Skill VI opening',['Scene safety','Question bystanders','Tap and shout “Are you OK?”','Tell Partner 2 to get the AED','Request additional EMS','Check breathing and carotid pulse 5–10 seconds','30 high-quality compressions'])]],
['AED use and ventilation: Skill VI steps 8–14',[
'**8.** Partner 2 **turns on the AED**. **9.** Follows prompts and **attaches the pads**. **10.** Directs Partner 1 to **stop CPR** and ensures everyone is **clear** during analysis; verbalizes “**All clear**” and **delivers the shock**. **11.** **Immediately directs Partner 1 to resume compressions**. **12.** Partner 2 moves **above the head**, opens the airway (head tilt–chin lift or jaw thrust), **suctions if needed** and **inserts an oral or nasal airway**, then seals the BVM mask—**narrow portion at the bridge of the nose**, a **“C”** with the thumb and index finger and the remaining three fingers forming an **“E”** to lift the jaw—and squeezes until the **chest rises**.',
'**13.** Partner 1 continues compressions at **30:2**, counting aloud. **14.** Interruptions are **no more than 5 seconds**; after about **2 minutes or 5 cycles**, the team **reassesses and switches roles**. **15–17.** Hand rub, communication and BSI. With an **advanced airway** placed by ALS, compressions become continuous with 1 breath every 6 seconds.',
order('AED and BVM',['Partner 2 turns on the AED','Attach pads and follow prompts','Clear everyone; “All clear”','Deliver shock','Resume compressions immediately','Insert OPA and ventilate with the E-C clamp','30:2, switch every 5 cycles'])]],
['When to transport and post-arrest care: Skill VI step 18',[
'**18.** The rating sheet asks the team to verbalize **transport** when **one** of these is met: **6–9 shocks delivered**, **3 consecutive “No Shock Advised” messages** or the patient **regains a pulse** (**return of spontaneous circulation, ROSC**). Local protocols may differ, and ALS may arrive to continue care. High-quality CPR on scene is usually more effective than CPR in a moving ambulance.',
'After **ROSC**, keep the AED pads on, check breathing and support ventilation (1 breath every 6 seconds if breathing is inadequate), give oxygen to keep SpO₂ around 94–99%, check blood pressure and treat hypotension per protocol, and transport to a hospital that can provide **post–cardiac arrest care**. Be ready for re-arrest. Ethical issues include valid **DNR orders** and obvious signs of death (such as decapitation or rigor mortis), when resuscitation is not started.',
chk('Which finding meets the HOSA criterion for verbalizing transport during Skill VI?',['Three consecutive “No Shock Advised” messages','One shock delivered','The first pulse check','Placing the pads'],'Also 6–9 shocks delivered or a regained pulse.')]],
['Other cardiovascular emergencies',[
'A **hypertensive emergency** (very high BP with headache, vision changes, confusion, chest pain or shortness of breath) can cause stroke or heart damage; give oxygen as needed and transport. An **aortic aneurysm or dissection** may cause **tearing** chest or back pain and unequal pulses or BPs between arms. **Cardiogenic shock** occurs when the heart cannot pump enough blood after a large heart attack.',
'Some patients have **implanted devices**: **pacemakers** and **implantable cardioverter-defibrillators (ICDs)**—place AED pads at least about 1 inch away. Patients with **left ventricular assist devices (LVADs)** may have no palpable pulse; ask family for the emergency instructions and contact the LVAD coordinator. Recognize rhythm patterns on a monitor even though EMTs do not interpret ECGs in most systems.',
{t:'w',kind:'ecg',title:'Recognize rhythms and artifacts',prompt:'Compare normal sinus rhythm with ventricular fibrillation, ventricular tachycardia and asystole. Which would an AED shock?'}]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Collapse at the gym',{a:{text:'A man collapses at a gym; a bystander says it happened 2 minutes ago. He is unresponsive, apneic and pulseless. (Scenario invented for practice.) What does Partner 1 do?',choices:[['Start 30 high-quality compressions while Partner 2 turns on the AED','b','Correct.',true],['Interview the bystander for 3 minutes','b','Start CPR first.',false]]},b:{text:'The AED advises a shock. Partner 2 says “All clear” and shocks. What next?',choices:[['Resume compressions immediately','c','Correct.',true],['Check the pulse for 30 seconds','c','Resume compressions.',false]]},c:{text:'After the second shock the patient regains a pulse. You support breathing, keep the pads on and transport.',end:true}})]],
["Cardiac medications EMTs may assist with",["**Aspirin** reduces platelet clumping and is given chewed (often 162–324 mg) for suspected ACS unless the patient is allergic, has active bleeding or protocols say otherwise. **Nitroglycerin** dilates blood vessels, reducing the heart’s workload; EMTs may **assist** with the patient’s own prescribed tablets or spray (usually 0.4 mg under the tongue, up to three doses 5 minutes apart) when systolic BP is above about 100 mm Hg and no erectile-dysfunction medications have been taken in the past 24–48 hours. Recheck BP after each dose—nitroglycerin can cause **hypotension** and headache.","Always confirm the medication rights, check expiration dates and follow medical direction. Document times, doses and the patient’s response, and report all medications to the hospital.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Give or withhold nitroglycerin?","buckets":["May assist (per protocol)","Withhold"],"items":[["Prescribed nitro, SBP 140, chest pain",0],["SBP 84 with chest pain",1],["Took an erectile-dysfunction drug yesterday",1],["Second dose 5 minutes later with SBP 128",0],["Nitro prescribed to a family member",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['ACS: chest pressure radiating to the arm or jaw, shortness of breath, sweating, nausea (atypical in women, older adults and diabetics); oxygen if SpO₂ under 94%, aspirin 162–324 mg chewed, assist with prescribed nitroglycerin if SBP above about 100.','Skill VI: scene safety, question bystanders, tap and shout, send for AED, request EMS, check breathing and carotid pulse 5–10 seconds, 30 compressions; Partner 2 powers on the AED, attaches pads, clears, shocks, resumes compressions, inserts OPA and ventilates with the E-C clamp.','Interruptions no more than 5 seconds, switch every 2 minutes or 5 cycles; transport after 6–9 shocks, 3 consecutive no-shock messages or ROSC; after ROSC keep pads on and support breathing.'],terms:[['Acute myocardial infarction','Death of heart muscle from a blocked coronary artery.'],['ROSC','Return of spontaneous circulation—a pulse returns after cardiac arrest.'],['Angina','Chest pain from inadequate blood flow to the heart.']],mcqs:[q('Which is a typical sign of acute myocardial infarction?','Crushing chest pressure radiating to the arm','Itchy rash','Ankle sprain','Hiccups only','Also sweating and nausea.'),q('Aspirin for suspected ACS is usually given:','162–324 mg, chewed','10 mg injected','2 g by IV','Never','If no allergy and per protocol.'),q('Who turns on the AED in Skill VI?','Partner 2','Partner 1','A bystander only','The judge','Partner 1 compresses.'),q('Per Skill VI, interruptions in compressions should be no more than:','5 seconds','30 seconds','1 minute','2 minutes','Minimize pauses.'),q('Transport is verbalized after how many shocks?','6–9','1','2','15','Or 3 no-shock messages or ROSC.'),q('In the E-C clamp, the narrow part of the mask goes:','At the bridge of the nose','On the chin','On the forehead','Over the eyes','Seal over nose and mouth.'),q('Where is an AED pad placed relative to an implanted pacemaker?','About 1 inch away','Directly over it','On the arm','It does not matter','Avoid the device.'),q('After ROSC, the AED pads should be:','Left in place','Removed immediately','Moved to the back','Thrown away','Re-arrest is possible.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how two EMTs manage an adult in cardiac arrest with an AED and BVM following HOSA Skill VI, including when to transport.',rubric:[['Recognition',['scene','bystanders','tap','shout','aed','ems','breathing','carotid','5','10 seconds']],['Compressions',['firm','flat','lower half','breastbone','arms straight','100-120','2 inches','recoil','count']],['AED and airway',['turn on','pads','clear','shock','resume','airway','opa','suction','e-c','chest rise','30:2']],['Team and transport',['5 seconds','switch','5 cycles','2 minutes','6-9 shocks','3','no shock','pulse','rosc']]],model:'We verify scene safety and question bystanders about what happened. Partner 1 taps the shoulder and shouts, “Are you OK?”, tells Partner 2 to start the AED and requests additional EMS, then checks for breathing and a carotid pulse for 5–10 seconds by scanning the chest and sliding two or three fingers into the groove beside the trachea. Finding the patient unresponsive, apneic and pulseless, Partner 1 kneels at the side, moves clothing, confirms the patient is on a firm, flat surface and gives 30 compressions with stacked hands on the lower half of the breastbone, arms straight, 100–120 per minute, at least 2 inches deep, counting aloud with full recoil. Partner 2 turns on the AED, attaches the pads following the prompts, directs Partner 1 to stop, says “All clear” to make sure no one is touching the patient and delivers the shock, then immediately tells Partner 1 to resume compressions. Partner 2 moves above the head, opens the airway, suctions if needed, inserts an oral airway and seals the BVM with the narrow end at the bridge of the nose using the E-C clamp, squeezing until the chest rises after every 30 compressions. We keep interruptions to 5 seconds or less and switch roles every 2 minutes or 5 cycles, and we verbalize transport when 6–9 shocks have been delivered, there are 3 consecutive “No Shock Advised” messages or the patient regains a pulse.'},sources:[RULES,AHA_CPR,AHA_HA]};
